Choosing a university is one of the biggest decisions you will make. A clear checklist helps you compare options fairly and avoid costly surprises.

## 1. Start with your goal

Think about the career you want and the qualification it needs. Choose the programme first, then look for universities that offer it well. A great university with the wrong programme is the wrong choice.

## 2. Check recognition

Recognition depends on the university and the programme. Before paying any fee, confirm how the qualification can be used for employment and further study in Oman, and whether any attestation or equivalency steps apply.

## 3. Compare entry requirements

Check the grades, subjects and English score each programme asks for. If you are close but not there yet, a [foundation year](/pathway-programs/foundation) or [IELTS preparation](/online-programs/ielts) may bridge the gap.

## 4. Work out the total cost

Tuition is only part of the cost. For study abroad, add accommodation, living expenses, travel, visa fees and health insurance. Ask whether instalments or scholarships are available.

## 5. Consider location and lifestyle

Studying in Oman or the GCC keeps you close to family. Studying abroad offers a different experience and international exposure. Think about climate, culture, language and how often you will travel home.

## 6. Note intakes and deadlines

Universities have set intake dates and application deadlines. Missing one can delay your plans by months, so build a timeline as soon as you have a shortlist.

## 7. Plan your skills too

Use the months before you start to build useful skills — English, Excel, AI tools or digital marketing — so you arrive ready to perform. Read [why a qualification works best with skills](/blog/qualification-plus-skills-what-employers-want).

